Write a self-contained prompt that instructs an AI coding agent to recreate a
Python script which deduplicates the PATH environment variable and writes an
auto-generated, marker-delimited block into a Termux ~/.bashrc file.
Create a Python 3 script intended for the Termux environment on Android (shebang
pointing to the Termux Python interpreter at
/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/env python).
The script must begin with `from __future__ import annotations` and use only the
standard library: `os`, `collections.OrderedDict`, and `pathlib.Path`.
Purpose: Remove duplicate directory entries from the shell PATH and persist the
deduplicated PATH into the user's Bash startup file, so that every new Bash
session in Termux uses a clean PATH.
Required functions and behavior: 1. get_current_path(): Return the value of the
PATH environment variable, or an empty string if it is not set. 2.
parse_path_values(path_string): If the input is empty or falsy, return an empty
list.
Otherwise split the string on the colon character ":" and return the resulting
list of entries. 3. deduplicate_paths(path_list): Preserve the first occurrence
order of each entry while removing later duplicates.
Skip empty entries entirely (empty strings are dropped).
Use a set for fast membership checks and an OrderedDict to keep insertion order.
Return a plain list. 4. generate_bash_config(paths): If the list is empty,
return a tuple of two empty strings `("", "")`.
Otherwise build: - A list of assignment lines, one per path, of the form
`P<n>="<path>"` where n starts at 1 and increments per path (use enumerate
starting at 1).
Paths are wrapped in double quotes without escaping. - A single export statement
that sets PATH to the references `$P1:$P2:...:$PN` followed by `:$PATH`, i.e.
`export PATH=$P1:...:$PN:$PATH`. - Return a tuple of (assignment list, export
statement string).
Note that the empty-case return is a two-element tuple of empty strings, while
the non-empty case returns a list and a string; preserve this inconsistency. 5.
read_bashrc(bashrc_path): Accept a pathlib.Path.
If the file exists, open it with UTF-8 encoding and return its full text;
otherwise return an empty string. 6. find_and_remove_old_config(content): Remove
any previously generated block from the given text.
The block is delimited by these exact marker lines (each followed by a newline):
- Start marker: `# === PATH DEDUPLICATION (AUTO-GENERATED) ===` - End marker: `#
=== END PATH DEDUPLICATION ===` Locate the start marker with a string find.
If found, search for the end marker starting from the start position.
If both are found, remove the text from the start marker through the end of the
end marker line and return the remaining content.
Handle missing markers gracefully by returning the content unchanged.
Also consider trimming surplus blank lines left behind so repeated runs do not
accumulate blank lines. 7.
Additional logic (main workflow, to be implemented in a main function guarded by
`if __name__ == "__main__":`): - Read the current PATH, parse it, and
deduplicate the entries. - Resolve the target file as the user's `~/.bashrc`
using `Path.home()`. - Read the existing bashrc content, strip any previously
generated deduplication block, and then append a new block containing the start
marker, the assignment lines, the export statement, and the end marker, each on
its own line and ending with a newline. - Write the combined content back to the
bashrc file with UTF-8 encoding, overwriting the file. - If the deduplicated
path list is empty, generate no new block (only remove the old one) and avoid
writing a malformed block. - Print a concise confirmation message to standard
output reporting how many unique PATH entries were written and the bashrc
location, and note that changes take effect in new shell sessions or after
sourcing the file.
Error handling and edge cases: - PATH unset or empty: produce no block and do
not fail. - Duplicate entries, empty segments from consecutive or trailing
colons: removed. - Missing ~/.bashrc: treat content as empty and create the
file. - Entries containing spaces or special characters: keep them as-is inside
the double quotes. - Running the script repeatedly must be idempotent: the old
block is removed before the new one is appended, so the file never accumulates
multiple blocks. - Do not add any external dependencies, do not invoke shell
commands, and do not modify environment variables of the running process.
Output the complete script with type hints optional, docstrings or comments
brief, and no third-party imports. ---
